  <XDFFLAG uniqueid="0xA078" flags="0x0">
    <title>DTC P1650 - Suzuki P1650 (warning-lamp code) - Warning lamp enabled</title>
    <description>Ticked = this DTC lights the Warning lamp when it sets (stock). Unticked = the code still sets and is stored, but no lamp lights. DTC index 119, lamp byte @0x1B9FD0 (mask 0x40).

HOW IT WORKS: each DTC has a 20-byte record in the diagnostic table at 0x1B9678 (131 records). Byte +0x0C of the record is the lamp group: 0x01 = MIL. FUN_938A8 moves the DTC into classes 6-8, which FUN_99E2A turns into the MIL request. 0x40 (or 0x80) = master warning lamp only (FUN_941AC). 0x00 = no lamp. Unticking the flag only stops the lamp. The DTC is still stored, still readable with a scan tool, and the ECU still runs its fail-safe for that fault (for example, P0500 still turns off traction control).</description>
    <CATEGORYMEM index="0" category="49" />
    <EMBEDDEDDATA mmedaddress="0x1B9FD0" mmedelementsizebits="8" mmedmajorstridebits="0" mmedminorstridebits="0" />
    <mask>0x40</mask>
  </XDFFLAG>
  <XDFFLAG uniqueid="0xA079" flags="0x0">
    <title>DTC P0602 - ECM programming error - none (group 0x10) enabled</title>
    <description>Ticked = this DTC lights the MIL when it sets (stock). Unticked = the code still sets and is stored, but no lamp lights. DTC index 120, lamp byte @0x1B9FE4 (mask 0x01). CAUTION: safety-critical fault (throttle-by-wire, ECM integrity or oil pressure). Hiding its lamp means you will not be told when the ECU has gone into a throttle or engine fail-safe. Not recommended. Stock lamp byte is 0x10, which already lights no lamp through these paths. Ticking this sets bit0, which turns the MIL on.

HOW IT WORKS: each DTC has a 20-byte record in the diagnostic table at 0x1B9678 (131 records). Byte +0x0C of the record is the lamp group: 0x01 = MIL. FUN_938A8 moves the DTC into classes 6-8, which FUN_99E2A turns into the MIL request. 0x40 (or 0x80) = master warning lamp only (FUN_941AC). 0x00 = no lamp. Unticking the flag only stops the lamp. The DTC is still stored, still readable with a scan tool, and the ECU still runs its fail-safe for that fault (for example, P0500 still turns off traction control).</description>
    <CATEGORYMEM index="0" category="49" />
    <EMBEDDEDDATA mmedaddress="0x1B9FE4" mmedelementsizebits="8" mmedmajorstridebits="0" mmedminorstridebits="0" />
    <mask>0x01</mask>
  </XDFFLAG>
  <XDFFLAG uniqueid="0xA07A" flags="0x0">
    <title>DTC P1622 - ECM EEPROM error - MIL enabled</title>
    <description>Ticked = this DTC lights the MIL when it sets (stock). Unticked = the code still sets and is stored, but no lamp lights. DTC index 121, lamp byte @0x1B9FF8 (mask 0x01). CAUTION: safety-critical fault (throttle-by-wire, ECM integrity or oil pressure). Hiding its lamp means you will not be told when the ECU has gone into a throttle or engine fail-safe. Not recommended.

HOW IT WORKS: each DTC has a 20-byte record in the diagnostic table at 0x1B9678 (131 records). Byte +0x0C of the record is the lamp group: 0x01 = MIL. FUN_938A8 moves the DTC into classes 6-8, which FUN_99E2A turns into the MIL request. 0x40 (or 0x80) = master warning lamp only (FUN_941AC). 0x00 = no lamp. Unticking the flag only stops the lamp. The DTC is still stored, still readable with a scan tool, and the ECU still runs its fail-safe for that fault (for example, P0500 still turns off traction control).</description>
    <CATEGORYMEM index="0" category="49" />
    <EMBEDDEDDATA mmedaddress="0x1B9FF8" mmedelementsizebits="8" mmedmajorstridebits="0" mmedminorstridebits="0" />
